    "issue": {
      "id": "9cb0acc8-c438-416f-9ddf-048fcfe1a882",
      "identifier": "SKI-11",
      "title": "Support skill groups and workspace member assignments",
      "url": "https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-11/support-skill-groups-and-workspace-member-assignments",
      "description": "## Executive summary\n\nAdd workspace-level skill groups so Skillplane can organize related skills by domain and associate workspace members with those groups—for example Design, Marketing, Development, and Sales.\n\n## Problem or opportunity\n\nA flat workspace skill catalog does not express which skills belong together or which members should work with a domain-specific collection. As the catalog and workspace membership grow, discovery, onboarding, and administration become harder.\n\n## Expected outcome\n\nWorkspace administrators can define reusable skill groups, associate skills and members with them, and inspect those relationships. Members can quickly find the skills relevant to their domains.\n\n## Goals\n\n* Create, rename, describe, archive, and restore skill groups within a workspace.\n* Associate skills with skill groups.\n* Associate workspace members with skill groups.\n* Show and filter skills and members by group.\n* Preserve workspace isolation, role-based administration, auditability, and deterministic API behavior.\n* Define how group membership interacts with skill visibility and authorization before rollout.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n* Replacing existing workspace roles.\n* Inferring a member's group from job title or behavior.\n* Automatically generating or changing skill content.\n* Implementing organization-wide groups shared across unrelated workspaces unless separately designed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n* A workspace can maintain domain groups such as Design, Marketing, Development, and Sales.\n* Authorized administrators can add and remove skills and members without duplicate associations.\n* Group detail views expose the associated skills and members; skill/member views expose their groups.\n* Lists and search can filter by group.\n* Archived groups do not silently delete skills, members, or historical audit records.\n* Cross-workspace references are rejected.\n* Every membership change is attributable in audit history.\n* Existing workspaces continue to work without creating groups.\n* Product and API semantics clearly state whether groups are organizational only or affect effective skill access.\n\n## Dependencies and risks\n\n* Existing workspace membership, skill visibility, authorization, and audit models.\n* Risk of accidentally widening access if group membership is treated as a grant.\n* Risk of confusing group membership with workspace role or team ownership.\n* Migration and indexing requirements for existing skills and members.\n* Large groups may require pagination and bulk-operation safeguards.\n\n## Open questions\n\n* Does membership grant skill access, configure defaults, or only categorize and filter?\n* Can a skill and a member belong to multiple groups?\n* Who may administer groups: owners only, or admins as well?\n* Should personal workspaces support groups?\n* How should group removal affect effective access, pinned skills, or agent configurations?\n* Are nested groups needed, or should the first version remain flat?\n\n## Supporting documents\n\n* [Ideation & Product Rationale](<https://linear.app/21n/document/ideation-and-product-rationale-ski-11-support-skill-groups-and-72f905b3b2ce>)\n* [Architecture & Delivery Plan](<https://linear.app/21n/document/architecture-and-delivery-plan-ski-11-support-skill-groups-and-daf45bcc1c17>)",
