{"i":1,"status":"fulfilled","value":{"chunk_id":"c8fcd7","wall_time_seconds":3.754130542,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":4804,"output":"{
  "successful": true,
  "data": {
    "composio_execution_message": null,
    "data": {
      "a": {
        "content": "Backlog issue: [SKI-11](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-11/support-skill-groups-and-workspace-member-assignments>)\n\n## Status\n\nThis is a proposed delivery model derived from the request. Exact current schema, API routes, UI components, and authorization helpers were not inspected for this backlog capture and must be verified before implementation.\n\n## Current-state constraints\n\n* Skillplane already has workspace tenancy, members, skills, visibility, authorization, audit, and immutable skill versions.\n* Groups must not weaken existing workspace or skill authorization.\n* Existing workspaces have no group records and must remain valid.\n* Exact role capabilities and current API/MCP extension points are **not yet defined** in this thread.\n\n## Proposed domain model\n\n### skill_groups\n\nProposed fields:\n\n* stable ID;\n* workspace ID;\n* name and optional description;\n* normalized name for workspace-local uniqueness if uniqueness is desired;\n* created/updated timestamps and actor attribution;\n* archived timestamp.\n\n### skill_group_skills\n\nProposed many-to-many association:\n\n* group ID;\n* skill ID;\n* created timestamp and actor;\n* unique constraint on group plus skill.\n\n### skill_group_members\n\nProposed many-to-many association:\n\n* group ID;\n* workspace-member ID;\n* created timestamp and actor;\n* unique constraint on group plus member.\n\nAll associations must enforce the same workspace boundary transactionally. Foreign keys should not cascade-delete canonical skills, users, or historical audit evidence when a group is archived.\n\n## Authorization model\n\n**Required invariant:** existing workspace membership and skill visibility checks run before group behavior.\n\n**Open question:** whether a group is organizational only or grants skill availability.\n\nRecommended sequencing:\n\n1. deliver organizational grouping with no authorization effect;\n2. separately specify an explicit access-policy mode and precedence rules if group-based grants are required;\n3. add explainability for effective access before enabling enforcement.\n\nPotential administrators are workspace owners and admins, but the exact role matrix must be confirmed against current policy.\n\n## API and MCP surface\n\n**Proposal:** expose deterministic, paginated operations for:\n\n* list/get/create/update/archive/restore groups;\n* list group skills and members;\n* add/remove skills and members idempotently;\n* filter skill/member listings by group;\n* optionally bulk-add/remove with bounded batch size and per-item results.\n\nExact route and tool names are **not yet defined**. Existing caller attribution, optimistic concurrency, idempotency keys, and audit conventions should be reused.\n\n## UI boundaries\n\n* Workspace settings: group lifecycle and membership management.\n* Skill catalog: group filters and group badges.\n* Skill detail: current groups.\n* Member detail: current groups.\n* Group detail: paginated skills, members, and recent changes.\n* Empty, archived, unauthorized, and partial-bulk states require explicit designs.\n\n## Data flow\n\n1. Authorized actor selects a workspace and group operation.\n2. Server resolves actor and workspace membership.\n3. Server validates all referenced skill/member records belong to the workspace.\n4. Transaction writes the group or association and durable audit event.\n5. Read models/search indexes are refreshed or invalidated.\n6. API returns canonical state; clients read back the result.\n\nIf groups later affect authorization, effective-access caches must be invalidated on every relevant membership or policy change.\n\n## Reliability and concurrency\n\n* Unique constraints prevent duplicate memberships.\n* Add/remove operations are idempotent.\n* Optimistic concurrency protects metadata edits and archive/restore races.\n* Bulk operations use bounded batches and explicit partial results or a transactionally all-or-nothing contract.\n* Pagination and stable ordering are required for large groups.\n* Archive/restore behavior must be race-safe.\n\n## Security and privacy\n\n* Reject cross-workspace IDs even if the actor can access both workspaces.\n* Do not expose private membership information through public skill discovery.\n* Preserve least privilege for administration.\n* Audit actor, operation, target IDs, source client, and outcome without storing secrets.\n* If access-bearing groups are added, deny-by-default precedence and regression tests are required.\n\n## Observability\n\nTrack:\n\n* group and membership mutation outcomes;\n* authorization denials;\n* cross-workspace rejection attempts;\n* bulk operation size and partial failures;\n* query latency and cardinality;\n* effective-access cache invalidation if introduced.\n\n## Migration and compatibility\n\n* Add nullable-independent tables; do not require backfilling existing skills or members.\n* Existing queries retain current behavior when no group filter is supplied.\n* No default groups should be inferred without an explicit product decision.\n* Rollback should disable new surfaces and preserve inert group data for a future retry; avoid destructive down-migrations in production.\n\n## Delivery sequence\n\n### Phase 1: semantics and data contracts\n\n* Decide organizational versus access-bearing behavior.\n* Confirm role matrix, uniqueness, multi-group, archive, and personal-workspace rules.\n* Define API/MCP contracts and audit events.\n\n### Phase 2: persistence and domain services\n\n* Add schema and constraints.\n* Implement workspace-safe lifecycle and association services.\n* Add unit, transaction, concurrency, and cross-workspace tests.\n\n### Phase 3: read surfaces\n\n* Add list/detail/filter APIs and indexes.\n* Add UI group settings, group detail, and skill/member badges.\n* Validate pagination, empty states, and accessibility.\n\n### Phase 4: bulk and operational hardening\n\n* Add bounded bulk workflows if required.\n* Add metrics, audit views, failure recovery, and performance tests.\n\n### Phase 5: optional access enforcement\n\n* Only after a separate approved policy specification.\n* Add effective-access calculation, explainability, cache invalidation, denial tests, and staged rollout.\n\n## Validation\n\n* Schema and migration tests.\n* Domain invariants and duplicate/idempotency tests.\n* Role matrix and cross-workspace denial tests.\n* API/MCP contract tests.\n* UI tests for create/edit/archive/filter and membership flows.\n* Audit read-back verification.\n* Performance tests for high-cardinality groups.\n* Regression tests proving ungrouped workspaces retain current behavior.\n\n## Risks and mitigations\n\n* **Accidental access widening:** keep first release organizational or require explicit deny-by-default policy.\n* **Role confusion:** distinguish roles, groups, and policies in UI and docs.\n* **Large association sets:** paginate and bound mutations.\n* **Stale discovery/access caches:** event-driven invalidation and read-after-write tests.\n* **Destructive lifecycle behavior:** archive groups and preserve associations/history.\n\n## Open questions\n\nThe product questions in the ideation document are blocking inputs for final architecture, especially access semantics, multiplicity, administrative roles, personal workspaces, and nesting.",
        "title": "Architecture & Delivery Plan — SKI-11: Support skill groups and workspace member assignments"
      },
      "b": {
        "content": "Backlog issue: [SKI-12](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-12/support-git-hosted-skill-sources-and-repository-wide-imports>)\n\n## Status\n\nThis is a proposed architecture based on the requested behavior and Skillplane's known immutable-version model. Exact repository services, database schema, provider abstractions, deployment topology, and UI code must be inspected before implementation.\n\n## Current-state constraints\n\n* Skillplane manages immutable skill bundles and published/candidate versions.\n* Existing amendment policy controls review or trusted publication.\n* Tenancy, visibility, authorization, audit, and contexts must remain authoritative.\n* A mutable Git branch cannot replace immutable Skillplane version identity.\n* Exact current Git integration capabilities are **not yet defined**.\n\n## Proposed component model\n\n### Git source registry\n\nA workspace-scoped source record representing a repository and connection policy.\n\nProposed fields:\n\n* source ID and workspace ID;\n* canonical provider/repository identity and display URL;\n* default ref strategy: pinned commit, tracked branch, or tracked tag;\n* credential connection reference for private access, never a raw secret;\n* discovery mode and optional manifest path;\n* sync policy and current status;\n* created/updated/archived timestamps and actor attribution.\n\n### Skill source binding\n\nConnects one managed skill to one path in a source.\n\nProposed fields:\n\n* source ID and skill ID;\n* repository-relative skill root or `SKILL.md` path;\n* optional per-binding ref override;\n* discovery key or upstream identity;\n* last successful commit and content digest;\n* drift/action state;\n* unique constraints preventing ambiguous duplicate bindings.\n\n### Sync run and per-binding result\n\nRecords one immutable synchronization attempt:\n\n* requested ref and resolved commit SHA;\n* trigger and actor;\n* start/end timestamps;\n* repository tree digest where useful;\n* per-skill added/changed/unchanged/missing/conflict/error outcomes;\n* candidate/version IDs created;\n* sanitized provider diagnostics.\n\n### Version provenance\n\nEvery source-created candidate/version should retain:\n\n* source ID;\n* repository identity and URL;\n* resolved commit SHA;\n* source path;\n* source content/bundle digest;\n* sync run ID;\n* import actor and policy outcome.\n\n## Synchronization pipeline\n\n 1. Authorize the actor for the workspace and source.\n 2. Resolve repository connection and credential reference.\n 3. Resolve the requested branch/tag/commit to an exact commit SHA.\n 4. Fetch a bounded tree or required paths through a constrained provider adapter.\n 5. Discover skill roots deterministically.\n 6. Validate paths, file types, sizes, manifests, bundle limits, and metadata.\n 7. Build the canonical Skillplane file set without executing repository code.\n 8. Compare source digests with current bindings and managed versions.\n 9. Produce a preview: add, update, unchanged, missing, renamed candidate, or conflict.\n10. On confirmed apply, create/import through existing immutable version and amendment-policy services.\n11. Persist sync results and provenance transactionally where possible.\n12. Read back created candidates/versions and update source health.\n\nA no-op comparison must not create a new version.\n\n## Single-file mode\n\n* User supplies source, ref policy, and one `SKILL.md` or skill-root path.\n* Validation confirms the path belongs to the resolved repository tree.\n* The binding maps exactly one managed skill to that path.\n* The Diffpanel example uses repository-relative path `skills/diffpanel-chapters/SKILL.md`; the canonical repository URL remains to be supplied.\n\n## Repository-wide mode\n\n**Proposal:** support a deterministic discovery precedence:\n\n1. explicit repository manifest, if defined;\n2. recognized skill metadata files;\n3. bounded recursive discovery of `SKILL.md` roots.\n\nThe final convention is **not yet defined**. Discovery must preview every candidate and flag duplicate slugs, nested roots, unsupported bundles, and conflicting existing bindings.\n\nOne source detail endpoint/view returns all bindings with path, managed skill, resolved commit, digest, sync result, and drift state.\n\n## Provider abstraction\n\nDefine a narrow interface for:\n\n* canonicalize repository identity;\n* verify access;\n* resolve ref to commit;\n* list bounded tree;\n* fetch bounded blobs;\n* register/verify webhooks if supported;\n* expose rate-limit and retry metadata.\n\nInitial provider selection is open. Public GitHub is a likely first candidate because the supplied collection example is GitHub-hosted, but this is a proposal, not a confirmed requirement.\n\n## Security and supply-chain controls\n\n* Never execute hooks, package scripts, binaries, or repository code.\n* Reject absolute paths, traversal, symlink escape, unsafe submodules, unsupported media, and decompression bombs.\n* Bound file count, per-file bytes, expanded bundle bytes, repository traversal depth, and total sync work.\n* Treat manifests and Markdown as untrusted data.\n* Store private-repository authorization as scoped credential references with rotation and revocation.\n* Redact tokens and provider headers from logs and audit.\n* Verify webhook signatures, timestamps, replay IDs, installation identity, and repository binding.\n* Require workspace and skill authorization at both preview and apply.\n* Preserve existing visibility rules; a public repository does not automatically make a managed skill public.\n* Surface commit verification/signature information as evidence if available, without treating it as sufficient trust on its own.\n\n## Concurrency and idempotency\n\n* Key sync application by source, resolved commit, discovery configuration, and content digest.\n* Use per-source serialization or optimistic concurrency to avoid racing webhook/manual runs.\n* Make apply replay-safe after timeouts.\n* Do not recreate successful candidates or bindings when recovering partial runs.\n* For repository-wide sync, persist per-binding results so failed items can be retried without duplicating successful items.\n\n## Rename and deletion handling\n\n* Missing upstream paths mark bindings as action required; they do not archive or delete managed skills automatically.\n* Rename detection may use explicit upstream IDs, manifest keys, or digest similarity, but requires confirmation when ambiguous.\n* Source removal disconnects future synchronization while preserving immutable versions and provenance.\n* Force-push or unreachable historical commits must be visible; previously recorded provenance remains immutable.\n\n## API/MCP surface\n\n**Proposal:** paginated, authorization-filtered operations for:\n\n* create/get/list/update/archive/restore sources;\n* validate connection and preview discovery;\n* list source bindings and grouped skills;\n* create/update/remove a binding;\n* start/get/list sync runs;\n* preview and apply repository-wide changes;\n* filter skills by source and source status.\n\nExact tool/route names and least-privilege scopes are **not yet defined**. Long-running sync should use asynchronous jobs with status polling or events rather than holding a request open.\n\n## Reliability and observability\n\nTrack:\n\n* source access and auth failures;\n* ref resolution latency and errors;\n* provider rate limit and retry state;\n* discovered/changed/unchanged/conflicting counts;\n* no-op ratio;\n* candidate/publication outcomes;\n* webhook signature/replay failures;\n* sync duration, queue age, partial failures, and recovery;\n* drift age per binding.\n\nAlerting should distinguish provider outage, expired credentials, malformed repository content, policy rejection, and internal persistence failure.\n\n## Migration and compatibility\n\n* Add source records and optional bindings without changing existing skills.\n* Existing manual publish/amend flows remain authoritative and available.\n* A manually managed skill is connected only through an explicit, confirmed operation.\n* Define behavior for manual amendments to a source-bound skill before launch: reject, allow as divergence, or support overlays.\n* Rollback disables synchronization and webhooks while preserving source records, bindings, runs, versions, and provenance.\n\n## Delivery sequence\n\n### Phase 1: source and provenance contracts\n\n* Decide providers, ref policies, discovery convention, credential model, and manual-edit behavior.\n* Define schema, provider interface, provenance, API/MCP contracts, and limits.\n* Threat-model public and private source ingestion.\n\n### Phase 2: public single-file source\n\n* Implement public repository access, commit resolution, constrained file fetch, validation, preview, binding, and manual sync.\n* Integrate changed content with existing amendment policy.\n* Deliver source provenance on skill/version views.\n\n### Phase 3: repository-wide discovery\n\n* Add deterministic discovery and preview.\n* Add bulk binding/apply with per-item idempotency and recovery.\n* Deliver source detail and filter/group views.\n\n### Phase 4: private sources and automation\n\n* Add scoped credential connections and rotation.\n* Add webhook and/or scheduled synchronization after deciding trigger policy.\n* Add signature validation, replay protection, rate-limit handling, queues, and retries.\n\n### Phase 5: hardening and ecosystem integration\n\n* Load/performance tests for large repositories.\n* Rename/transfer/fork handling.\n* [skills.sh](<http://skills.sh>) integration only after its role and contract are defined.\n* Operational dashboards, alerts, and recovery tooling.\n\n## Validation\n\n* Unit tests for URL canonicalization, ref resolution, path validation, discovery, digesting, and provenance.\n* Contract tests against supported provider APIs.\n* Security tests for traversal, symlinks, submodules, oversized files, malicious manifests, secret redaction, and webhook replay.\n* Idempotency and concurrency tests for repeated/racing syncs.\n* Integration tests from repository commit to candidate/version and policy outcome.\n* Repository-wide partial-failure and recovery tests.\n* Cross-workspace and private-source authorization denial tests.\n* UI tests for source setup, preview, apply, source grouping, drift, and failure recovery.\n* Production canary with a controlled public repository before broader rollout.\n\n## Risks and mitigations\n\n* **Supply-chain compromise:** immutable commit provenance, review policy, no execution, and visible diffs.\n* **Credential leakage:** provider connections, least scopes, redaction, rotation, and audit.\n* **Mutable refs/force-pushes:** resolve every run to a commit and preserve prior provenance.\n* **Provider outages/rate limits:** queueing, backoff, status distinction, and manual retry.\n* **Mass unintended changes:** preview, bounded batches, policy gates, and non-destructive deletion handling.\n* **Layout ambiguity:** explicit manifest precedence and conflict states.\n* **State divergence:** define manual-edit/overlay behavior before source binding launch.\n\n## Open questions\n\nProvider scope, ref semantics, trigger modes, repository layout, overlay behavior, rename/deletion policy, source transfer identity, and [skills.sh](<http://skills.sh>) integration are blocking design decisions for later phases.",
