ds.\n* Groups complement workspace roles; they do not replace owner, admin, editor, or viewer semantics.\n* Membership is explicit. Skillplane should not infer it from titles, activity, or private data.\n* Archive is preferred over destructive deletion when relationships or audit history exist.\n* Existing workspaces remain valid with zero groups.\n\n## Ideas discussed and product options\n\n### Option A: organizational grouping only\n\nGroups drive labels, navigation, filtering, and onboarding but do not change authorization.\n\n* Lower security risk and simpler initial delivery.\n* Does not solve cases where group membership is intended to grant access.\n\n### Option B: group-based skill availability\n\nA member's groups determine which workspace skills are available to them.\n\n* Stronger delegation and least-privilege potential.\n* Creates a new authorization layer and requires careful precedence rules.\n\n### Option C: separate organization and access controls\n\nA group organizes skills and members, while an explicit policy flag or separate access policy determines whether it grants availability.\n\n* More expressive and safer long term.\n* Higher conceptual and implementation complexity.\n\n**Open question:** which option reflects the intended first release? The request supports organization and member association, but does not establish access-control semantics.\n\n## Desired outcomes and success measures\n\n* Administrators can create domain groups and maintain skill/member associations without duplicates.\n* Members find relevant skills with fewer catalog searches.\n* Group filters and detail views return consistent results across UI and API surfaces.\n* No group operation leaks or links cross-workspace records.\n* Group archival and membership changes remain auditable.\n* Existing workspaces and skills require no migration action to continue working.\n* If groups affect authorization, permission tests show no accidental access widening.\n\nPotential quantitative measures are not yet defined. Candidate measures include time to onboard a member, skill discovery success, percentage of skills organized, and failed/unauthorized group mutations.\n\n## Product and UX considerations\n\n* Group names should be clear within a workspace; uniqueness rules need definition.\n* Group cards or rows should show skill and member counts.\n* Skill and member detail views should expose group membership.\n* Filtering should support one or multiple groups if many-to-many is adopted.\n* Empty groups should be valid during setup.\n* Archived groups should disappear from normal selection while remaining recoverable.\n* Bulk assignment may be important for large workspaces but should not obscure per-item failures.\n* UI language must distinguish “workspace role,” “skill group,” and “access policy.”\n\n## Operational considerations\n\n* Every create, rename, archive, restore, and association change should be attributable.\n* Bulk changes need bounded request sizes, idempotency, and clear partial-failure handling.\n* Group membership counts and filters need pagination at scale.\n* Support tooling should make effective membership and, if applicable, effective access explainable.\n\n## Goals\n\n* Workspace-level skill groups.\n* Skill-to-group and member-to-group associations.\n* Bidirectional inspection and filtering.\n* Safe lifecycle and audit behavior.\n* Explicit authorization semantics before production rollout.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n* Replacing workspace roles.\n* Automatic group inference.\n* Cross-workspace or organization-global groups in the initial scope.\n* Nested group hierarchies unless separately approved.\n* Automatic modification of skill content.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n* Domain groups such as Design, Marketing, Development, and Sales can be represented.\n* Authorized administrators can add and remove skills and members idempotently.\n* Group, skill, and member views expose consistent associations.\n* Search/list operations can filter by group.\n* Cross-workspace associations fail safely.\n* Archiving a group does not delete skills, members, or audit history.\n* Existing ungrouped workspaces continue to work.\n* Documentation and UI clearly state whether membership is organizational or access-bearing.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n* A workspace is the tenancy boundary.\n* Existing skill and member identifiers remain canonical.\n* Existing workspace roles continue to govern who may administer workspace configuration.\n\n## Open questions\n\n* Does group membership grant access, set defaults, or only categorize?\n* Can members and skills belong to multiple groups?\n* Can admins manage groups, or only owners?\n* Are groups available in personal workspaces?\n* Are nested groups needed later?\n* How should removing a member from a group affect active agent sessions or cached discovery?\n* Is bulk assignment required for the first release?",
        "title": "Ideation & Product Rationale — SKI-11: Support skill groups and workspace member assignments"
      },
      "b": {
        "content": "Backlog issue: [SKI-12](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-12/support-git-hosted-skill-sources-and-repository-wide-imports>)\n\n## Context and motivating problem\n\nSkill authors often keep canonical `SKILL.md` content in Git. Manually copying that content into Skillplane creates drift, loses source provenance, and turns upstream updates into repetitive publishing work.\n\nThe request has two related modes:\n\n1. one managed skill uses one Git-hosted skill file as its source of truth;\n2. one repository acts as a shared source for multiple managed skills, with a source view showing every associated skill.\n\nExamples supplied in the discussion:\n\n* Diffpanel file: `skills/diffpanel-chapters/SKILL.md` in the Diffpanel repository;\n* [vercel-labs/skills](<https://github.com/vercel-labs/skills>) as a repository-scale skill collection;\n* [skills.sh](<https://skills.sh/>) as an ecosystem/catalog example.\n\nThe exact public Diffpanel repository URL was not supplied; only the repository-relative path is treated as confirmed.\n\n## Target users and stakeholders\n\n* Product teams that publish an official skill alongside their application code.\n* Skill collection maintainers managing many skills in one repository.\n* Workspace administrators connecting and monitoring external sources.\n* Skill consumers who need trustworthy provenance and freshness.\n* Security and operations teams responsible for repository credentials, supply-chain risk, sync reliability, and audits.\n\n## Core user journeys\n\n### Connect one official skill file\n\nA Diffpanel maintainer connects a repository, chooses an allowed ref policy and `skills/diffpanel-chapters/SKILL.md`, previews the imported content, then creates or links the managed Skillplane skill. Later upstream changes become reviewable Skillplane candidates or versions according to publication policy.\n\n### Import a skill repository\n\nA maintainer registers a collection repository once. Skillplane discovers supported skill roots, previews additions/updates/conflicts, and creates explicit bindings from each managed skill to the common source.\n\n### Inspect source provenance\n\nA user opens a source page and sees all managed skills derived from it, their source paths, resolved commit SHAs, content digests, last sync results, drift, and failures.\n\n### Recover from upstream changes\n\nIf a file is renamed, deleted, malformed, or conflicts with another binding, Skillplane reports a non-destructive action state rather than silently deleting or overwriting managed skills.\n\n## Product principles\n\n* Git is the authoring source; Skillplane remains the immutable distribution, authorization, review, audit, and context control plane.\n* Every imported version must resolve to an immutable commit, even when tracking a mutable branch.\n* Repository content is untrusted input and is never executed during import.\n* Sync behavior must be previewable, idempotent, attributable, and recoverable.\n* One shared source object should support both single-file and repository-scale modes.\n* Existing manually managed skills do not change until explicitly connected.\n\n## Product options and tradeoffs\n\n### Ref strategy\n\n* **Commit pin:** maximally reproducible, but updates require explicit ref changes.\n* **Tracked branch/tag:** convenient continuous updates, but every sync must resolve and record a commit.\n* **Proposal:** support both, with branch tracking producing immutable commit-backed sync runs.\n\n### Sync trigger\n\n* Manual sync is simplest and easiest to reason about.\n* Scheduled polling avoids provider-specific webhook setup but costs API quota.\n* Webhooks improve freshness but require installation lifecycle and replay protection.\n* **Proposal:** manual first, then configurable webhook or schedule after reliability is proven.\n\n### Repository discovery\n\n* Recursive `SKILL.md` discovery is convenient but may be ambiguous in large monorepos.\n* A repository manifest is deterministic but adds setup.\n* Reusing existing `skill.json` metadata may cover some repositories.\n* **Open question:** define a precedence order and preview before creating bindings.\n\n### Update policy\n\n* Auto-publish is fast but inappropriate for untrusted or broad changes.\n* Existing Skillplane amendment/review policy can decide whether a changed source becomes a candidate or publication.\n* **Proposal:** source sync feeds the existing policy instead of creating a parallel publishing path.\n\n## Desired outcomes and success measures\n\n* Teams can connect official Git skill files without maintaining duplicate source text.\n* Every Skillplane version can show source repository, path, and commit provenance.\n* Repository-scale sources expose a complete, filterable list of associated skills.\n* No-op synchronization creates no duplicate versions.\n* Sync failures are visible and recoverable without data loss.\n* Import does not execute code or leak repository credentials.\n* Existing publication review and authorization guarantees remain intact.\n\nCandidate quantitative measures include sync success rate, drift duration, no-op rate, mean recovery time, duplicate-version count, and percentage of imported versions with complete provenance.\n\n## UX considerations\n\n* Source creation should validate URL, provider, access, ref, and discovery before saving.\n* A preview should separate additions, updates, unchanged skills, removals, renames, and conflicts.\n* Source status should distinguish connected, syncing, current, drifted, action required, auth failed, and unreachable.\n* Skill detail should show source provenance and whether local edits or overlays are allowed.\n* Source detail should group all associated skills and expose per-skill paths/results.\n* Removing a source must explain that immutable skill history remains.\n\n## Operational considerations\n\n* Private-repository credentials require explicit scope, rotation, revocation, and redacted audit behavior.\n* Provider rate limits, webhook replay, force-pushes, and deleted refs must be observable.\n* Bulk sync needs concurrency limits, resumability, and per-skill results.\n* Repository transfer or rename should preserve a stable source identity where possible.\n* Source health must not be inferred solely from a successful webhook receipt.\n\n## Goals\n\n* Single-file Git source of truth.\n* Repository-wide discovery/import for multiple skills.\n* Common source grouping and provenance views.\n* Deterministic immutable commit resolution.\n* Secure, review-policy-aware synchronization.\n* Non-destructive drift and failure handling.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n* Arbitrary repository code execution.\n* General-purpose Git mirroring.\n* Silent deletion when upstream content disappears.\n* Assuming one repository layout.\n* Replacing immutable Skillplane versions with mutable remote reads.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n* Repository URL, ref policy, and skill path can configure a single binding.\n* Each successful sync records commit SHA, path, digest, timestamp, and outcome.\n* Repository discovery is deterministic and previewed.\n* A source page lists all associated managed skills and statuses.\n* Same commit plus content is idempotent.\n* Changed content follows existing review/publication policy.\n* Rename, deletion, malformed content, and conflicts are explicit and non-destructive.\n* Untrusted-content boundaries prevent execution, path escape, oversized bundles, unsupported files, and secret exposure.\n* Manual skills remain unchanged until connected.\n* Source removal preserves immutable history.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n* Git repositories can be resolved through provider APIs or a constrained fetch layer.\n* Current immutable bundle/version and amendment policy remain authoritative.\n* A source and its bindings are workspace-scoped.\n\n## Open questions\n\n* Initial Git providers and authentication methods.\n* Supported URL forms and self-hosted Git.\n* Branch/tag/commit behavior.\n* Manual, scheduled, or webhook sync triggers.\n* Discovery manifest and directory conventions.\n* Rename/deletion confirmation.\n* Overlay or local-edit support.\n* Repository fork/transfer identity.\n* The role of [skills.sh](<http://skills.sh>): catalog, resolver, import partner, or reference only.",
        "title": "Ideation & Product Rationale — SKI-12: Support Git-hosted skill sources and repository-wide imports"
